.taskmaster\docs\architecture-notes.md:104: - **Member management** — **no** add/remove/invite tools. So automated volunteer offboarding is NOT possible (resolves the old `[VERIFY]`) → the NGO removes the volunteer manually at completion. ai4good is never a member of the NGO's Lovable workspace (seat dropped, #2 below).
.taskmaster\docs\architecture-notes.md:209: - **Role model (verified against Lovable's full workspace role matrix, 2026-07-13).** From the matrix: *Create new projects in a workspace* = Editor **Yes**; *Manage Supabase* (enable/manage the DB) = Editor **Yes**; *Manage GitHub* = Editor Yes; *Publish* = Editor Yes; *Manage plans & billing* = Editor **No**; *Manage Integrations* (workspace-level connectors) = Editor **No**; *View usage* = Editor **No**. Only *Disconnect Supabase* (destructive, project-level) is admin/owner. So the volunteer is invited as **Editor** and does the **entire technical setup**; the NGO's role collapses to account/billing. (The PRD states these as capabilities — "build-only member with no billing access", "read-only monitoring account with no billing or admin access" — the verified Lovable mechanism for both is the **Editor** role; d56.) Kickoff split:
.taskmaster\docs\architecture-notes.md:210:   - **NGO owner (account/billing only):** create workspace + plan + card; **set the volunteer's per-member `monthly_credit_limit`** (native Lovable spend cap, UI-only, no MCP setter); invite the volunteer as **Editor**; add a workspace-level connector only if a specific project needs one (*Manage Integrations* is admin/owner).
.taskmaster\docs\architecture-notes.md:214: - **Credit-display read mechanism — server-side bot (INTENDED, conditional on the `[verify]` below; founder 2026-07-13, d50).** Preferred over the volunteer's in-session read, which is fragile and dev-machine-dependent. Design: a dedicated **ai4good bot Lovable account** is an **editor** member of each NGO workspace; a **server runs headless Claude Code** with the Lovable MCP authed once (browser) as the bot; a **skill loops `get_workspace` over the active-project `workspace_id` list**, computes OK/low/exhausted, and POSTs to an ai4good edge function → per-project cache → UI (REQ-010). Why it's buildable despite Lovable exposing no server/machine auth (OAuth-only, whitelisted clients, no API keys — verified): **Claude Code is a whitelisted OAuth client**, so headless Claude Code is a *supported* client, not a custom server (which Lovable blocks). Editor role keeps billing locked. **Invites + gate (d51):** the **NGO performs both invites** — the ai4good read-only bot (editor) *and* the volunteer (editor). Not the volunteer or an operator: the headless bot has **no way to invite anyone** (no member-management MCP verb — inviting is dashboard-only; an editor *account* has the permission, but only a human at the dashboard can exercise it, and the bot is headless). **Both memberships are a hard kickoff gate** (folds into the REQ-024 "Lovable setup pending" blocker / REQ-005.5 kickoff): the volunteer cannot start and project state cannot progress to active build until both are confirmed — so the platform is **never building blind** to Lovable credit consumption + state (the monitoring bot is provably present before credits are spent). Confirmation: the **bot self-confirms** via a successful `get_workspace`; the **volunteer's** membership is confirmed via their own session / validated paste-back (`[verify]` — no member-list API to enumerate members). The read-only editor bot **bounds** platform visibility to exactly credit-consumption + state (least-privilege). Offboarding removes both (dashboard). A Lovable **member-management API** would remove both manual invites — part of the durable ask.
.taskmaster\docs\architecture-notes.md:217:   - **Pure-doc MEMBERSHIP fold — DONE (d52, 2026-07-13).** Scrubbed the now-false "ai4good is never a member / nothing to remove / no member seat" language across the PRD (REQ-008, REQ-010, REQ-012, REQ-021, REQ-005.5, Story 6, s9 risk). The PRD now states: ai4good runs a **read-only monitoring account** (editor role, no billing/admin) in each NGO workspace; the **NGO invites both** the volunteer and the monitoring account at kickoff, **both validated before the volunteer starts**; **offboarding removes both**; credit status is read via the monitoring account (not the volunteer's session). What stays in these notes (NOT in the PRD): the **read MECHANISM** — headless Claude Code as the monitoring account — which is still conditional on the `[verify — make-or-break]` token-persistence proof above. If that proof fails and the bot is dropped, the membership statements revert.
.taskmaster\docs\architecture-notes.md:218:   - `[still pending]` a roadmap item for the **durable Lovable service-account / API-key + member-management API** (formalize as an RM referenced from the pure doc to keep the RM bijection) — the headless-Claude-Code setup is the interim. Raise that ask with Lovable in parallel (their docs invite "contact support for alternative authentication").
.taskmaster\docs\prd-mvp.md:418: - Discovery recommends Lovable with rationale and no project-specific dollar estimate; the scope doc states that Lovable is paid directly, never from fuel, and carries the standard ~$25/month Lovable subscription disclosure (REQ-004, Promise §10) — the one dollar figure it must show. The NGO is reminded before kickoff to set up the workspace and invite the volunteer.
.taskmaster\docs\prd-mvp.md:420:   1. **NGO (account + billing only):** creates the workspace, funds it, sets the volunteer's credit cap, and invites two members — the volunteer as a **build-only member with no billing access**, and **ai4good's read-only monitoring account** (no billing or admin access; it can only observe, surfacing Lovable credit consumption and workspace state to the platform).
.taskmaster\docs\prd-mvp.md:437: - [ ] The NGO invites both the volunteer and ai4good's read-only monitoring account at kickoff; both memberships are validated before the volunteer starts.
.taskmaster\docs\prd-mvp.md:502: **Pull-based workflow:** the pull operates on the PM TREE — pulling a requirement item is the commitment signal (marking it in progress) and binds attribution to it; volunteers pull the next unblocked requirement and the coordinator never pushes. PM items carry their OWN blocking relations (seeded from the scope doc's dependencies); a PM requirement is pullable when its PM-level blockers are done — dev-tree relations never determine PM pullability. Within a pulled requirement, the volunteer works its dev-tree items with vendor-native tooling at their own discretion. Norms: one PM requirement in progress (one per worktree if parallel), pull before starting, comment when blocked, never move PM-tree status by hand. Onboarding: match → invite → browse the PM tree (before cloning anything) → the first work session connects task access → the first pull activates attribution. An in-progress issue with no branch activity for N days raises a coordinator flag proposing return to the backlog. Agentic loops are permitted (handling is loop-agnostic); the template ships a reviewer agent; a loop degrades on auth failure and its PRs never auto-merge. **[DECISION: OD-1 — reviewer identity + merge authority, per project.]**
.taskmaster\docs\prd-mvp.md:801: - **Q7, platform-level Lovable usage visibility — resolved in v1; one vendor ask open:** the platform reads each workspace's credit status through its own read-only monitoring account (REQ-021), so visibility no longer depends on the volunteer's session. Still open with Lovable: a durable service-account / API-key and a member-management API — today the platform's read depends on an interactive sign-in and all workspace invites/removals are manual dashboard actions.
.taskmaster\docs\prd-new.md:141: - **Single-seat NGO in v1 (decision-28):** one NGO = one account. The org contact who signed up performs every NGO-side action: funding, acknowledgments, scope edits, handoff sign-off. Teammate invites, per-NGO roles, and multi-NGO membership are v1.5 (the two-layer authorization model stays in the schema, so v1.5 adds rows, not migrations). Guards (attached by the decision-28 review; all cheap): the acknowledgment moments capture name + title + an explicit authority attestation (authorized to bind the NGO, fund non-refundable fuel, accept no-SLA terms, approve handoff); org-controlled email is preferred and shared credentials are prohibited in ToS/UI copy (acknowledgments are per named human); an audited platform-admin contact-transfer/recovery action exists (out-of-band verification → move NGO ownership to a new account, deactivate the old, preserve history); one non-login secondary escalation contact is captured at concierge onboarding.
.taskmaster\docs\prd-new.md:319: - **Lovable path (v1 default):** the platform does not pre-create the repo at funding. Kickoff auto-raises a Lovable-setup blocker, and the NGO (workspace + admin invite) and volunteer (GitHub install + connector + paste-back) work an 11-step checklist that the platform auto-validates, recording the repo URL — typically same-day, with aging escalation if either party stalls. No platform-admin involvement. The repo URL is a hard handoff precondition.
.taskmaster\docs\prd-new.md:416: 1. Discovery flags whether Lovable is recommended, with rationale — no dollar estimate. The scope doc shows a labeled note: paid directly to Lovable, never deducted from fuel. Pre-kickoff, the NGO sees a reminder banner to set up the workspace and invite the volunteer.
.taskmaster\docs\prd-new.md:417: 2. Setup is mandatory at kickoff: a blocking setup item is auto-raised to the NGO. The NGO and volunteer complete it through the existing blocker + comment surfaces; the platform admin has zero per-project involvement, and the NGO needs no GitHub account. The checklist: the NGO signs up (a paid Lovable tier is required for the workspace-admin role), creates the workspace/project seeded from a one-click-copied scope summary, and invites the volunteer as workspace admin **plus the platform's ops account as a member (decision-35 — one more one-click invite; this seat powers automated offboarding at handoff/abandonment and exits at handoff)**; the volunteer accepts, connects Lovable's GitHub sync so the repo is created inside the platform's GitHub org, and pastes back the workspace/repo identifiers; the platform auto-validates (repo in the platform org, repo flipped public after validation, platform GitHub App installed, name collisions surfaced, volunteer repo permission normalized so they cannot add collaborators unnoticed), auto-resolves the item, seeds the repo template, and notifies both parties.
.taskmaster\docs\prd-new.md:509: **Provisioning — pool model:** Linear workspace creation has no public API, so the concierge pre-creates a pool of ready workspaces in batch, each pre-wired for platform access, events, and GitHub integration. Kickoff then needs no manual step: assign the next ready workspace, rename it for the project, invite the volunteer, and seed the PRD bootstrap task (REQ-036 — the build tree arrives post-gate). A replenishment task fires when the ready pool drops below a watermark (default 3); an empty pool at kickoff raises an external-dependency blocker — the only stall mode. Honest note: bulk-provisioned empty workspaces sharpen — not soften — the fair-use question. The written blessing from Linear is POSTPONED to a pool scale-out gate (founder verdict, flow #20 2026-07-08): the pilot's handful of workspaces proceeds on ordinary free-tier terms, risk accepted, with the paid tier as the fallback if Linear ever objects.
.taskmaster\docs\prd-new.md:513: **Pull-based workflow:** self-assignment is the commitment signal — it flips the NGO panel to "in progress." Volunteers pull the next unblocked issue; the coordinator never pushes assignments. The norms: one issue in progress at a time (one per worktree if parallel); assign before starting; comment when blocked; never move status by hand. Onboarding: match → workspace invite → the volunteer browses session-sized briefs before cloning anything → the first work session connects task access once → the first pull activates attribution (REQ-034). An in-progress issue with no branch activity for N days raises a coordinator flag proposing release back to the backlog. Agentic loops are permitted — governance is loop-agnostic. The repo template ships a skeptical reviewer-agent default; loops must degrade on auth failure (queue updates, surface at session end); loop PRs are never auto-merged. **[DECISION: OD-1 — reviewer identity + merge authority per project.]**
.taskmaster\docs\prd-new.md:515: **Write authority (real-signals enforcement):** volunteers and their agents may read, comment, and self-assign — never change status. The GitHub integration moves status on PR events. The platform creates issues (decomposition; in v1, scope-addition tasks are volunteer-created per REQ-025), invites members, reverts, and cancels. The NGO holds no Linear seat — its visibility is the Status Panel exclusively. Because OAuth scopes cannot express "assign + comment but not status," enforcement is detect-and-revert: any status change not made by the GitHub integration, and lacking a linked merged PR, is automatically reverted with an explanatory comment and a low-tone notification to the volunteer (a restricted read/assign/comment proxy is held in reserve if reverting proves noisy). Agent actions are attributed to the volunteer — human vs agent is indistinguishable and accepted; the volunteer owns their agents' actions.
.taskmaster\docs\prd-new.md:743: - **Phase 5 — Public Beta & Concierge Launch (weeks 14-16):** Launch checklist, invite-only → open rollout, internal goals analytics, concierge supply-funnel tooling, aging-open-project nudges, hand-match tool for the first ~10-15 projects, v1.5 spec + backlog prioritization + retrospective. Exit: 10+ projects in flight, ≥1 public-beta handoff, and ≥1 handed-off project still reachable at its live URL 30 days post-handoff.
.taskmaster\docs\prd-new.md:800: - **35 — Platform Lovable member seat; offboarding automated (2026-07-09, founder).** The setup checklist adds one invite: the platform's ops account joins each project's Lovable workspace as a build-phase member. That seat powers automated volunteer removal at handoff, abandonment, and AUP deactivation — no waiting on the NGO — and at handoff the platform removes itself ("last one out"), so post-handoff the NGO holds the workspace alone with zero platform access. Narrow exception to the no-Lovable-access posture: the seat reads no usage/billing data and exists only for member management; exposure during build is bounded by the Tier-2 fixtures-only rule. [VERIFY on the first pilot]: member add/remove/self-remove via Lovable API/MCP; fallback = the manual NGO-removal path with a notice.
.taskmaster\docs\prd.md:411: - [ ] NGO admins can invite teammates to their org (email invitation, role assignment).
.taskmaster\docs\prd.md:478: **Task Breakdown:** Small (4h schema + RLS), Small (4h invite UI), Small (3h tests).
.taskmaster\docs\prd.md:687: **Provisioning failure handling:** if any of the above fails mid-sequence, project stays in `in_progress` (no `provisioning` sub-state; failures surface as blockers or the pending ops task). The volunteer is gated from work on whichever resource is pending (e.g., no backlog to pull until pool assignment + decomposition push complete — pool-empty is the only workspace stall mode; the Lovable invite comes from the NGO, outside ai4good). This avoids state-machine bloat while keeping the operational status visible via REQ-024 blockers — codex round 2 #6 resolved this way rather than adding a new state.
